===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|elevenses}}

# {{lb|en|Britain|informal}} A [[short#Adjective|short]] [[mid-morning]] [[break#Noun|break]] taken around [[eleven]] [[o'clock]] for a [[drink#Noun|drink]] or [[light#Adjective|light]] [[snack#Noun|snack]].
#: {{synonyms|en|elevensies|q1=one sense}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Anna Lee|title=The Pinch of Salt|magazine=The Ladies’ Companion: A Monthly Magazine, Embracing Literature and the Arts,{{nb...|Embellished with Engravings, and Music Arranged for the Piano Forte, Guitar, &c.}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Published by William W. Snowdon|month=February|year=1837|volume=VI|page=162|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=tXNAAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA162|column=2|oclc=6444302|passage=The harvestmen who board in the farm-houses fare sumptuously during the month of harvest.— {{...}} at eleven o'clock in the morning, and four in the afternoon, they have each a large light plum-bun, with a pint of ale a piece, carried into the field, to encourage them to work cheerfully. These extra refreshments they call, in their provincial language, their "'''''elevenses'''''" and "''fourses''." I could not at first imagine what the servants meant by talking of carrying the harvestmen their '''elevenses''' and fourses, till Mrs. Henley explained that it was a vulgar abbreviation of the four-o'clock and eleven o'clock meals.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Khushwant Singh|authorlink=Khushwant Singh|chapter=Man, How the Government of India Run!|title=The Collected Short Stories of Khushwant Singh|location=New Delhi|publisher=Ravi Dayal Publisher|year=1989|page=96|isbn=978-817530044-6|chapter2=Book Extract: They also Serve&nbsp;…|title2=Sunday|location2=Calcutta|publisher2=[[w:ABP Group|Ananda Bazar Patrika]]|year2=2 January – 2 February 1991|volume2=18|issue2=4|pages2=75–76|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.99395/page/n437|oclc2=9353987|passage="In Europe," said Ghosh Babu who read a great deal, "they all stop work at eleven to have tea or coffee or cocoa, or some stronger beverage; they call it '''elevenses'''." / {{...}} ["]Ghosh Babu, how long time they get for '''elevenses'''?" / "About half-an-hour," replied Ghosh with authority. {{...}} / "We get no time off for '''elevenses'''," complained Sambamurthy. "We should bring it up before the Clerks' Association."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Simon Majumdar|authorlink=Simon Majumdar|chapter=Elevenses|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gafeRHHcDz4C&pg=PT52|title=Eating for Britain: A Journey into the Heart (and Belly) of the Nation|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publisher)|John Murray]]|year=2010|isbn=978-1-84854-226-6|passage=Even in these tough economic times when many employers seem hell-bent on sucking any and all vestiges of pleasure out of the daily existence of the average working stiff, some things remain sacrosanct. Chief among them is the partaking of '''elevenses'''. Even if we spend the majority of our lives glued to a desk, staring at a computer screen or in meetings pretending we care about the interpretation of a spreadsheet, the break for a hot drink and a little snackette between breakfast and lunch is a covenant between Britain and God.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 January 13|author=Christian Wolmar|title=Read all about London's Cathedrals of Steam|journal=RAIL, issue 922|page=63|text=Betjeman, who was a bit of a gricer, wrote: "I know of no greater pleasure for '''elevenses''' in London than to sit in this tea place and watch the trains arrive and depart."}}

